<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Republic of Cuba</h1><p class="st-meta">1959 CE – present · capital Havana</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Revolutionary Cuba since 1959, the only communist state in the Americas, ruled by Fidel and Raúl Castro and their successors for over six decades.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fidel-castro">Fidel Castro's</a> 26th of July Movement overthrew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fulgencio-batista">Fulgencio Batista</a>, who fled on 1 January 1959. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-castro">Castro</a> became prime minister and launched land reform and nationalisation of American and Cuban businesses. The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a> imposed an embargo, and Castro declared the revolution socialist in April 1961, during the failed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-pigs-invasion">Bay of Pigs invasion</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> allied with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, and the stationing of Soviet missiles caused the crisis of October 1962. The Communist Party, founded in 1965, rules as the sole party under the 1976 and 2019 constitutions. The state provided free health care and education while suppressing dissent, and over a million Cubans emigrated, many in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mariel-boatlift">Mariel boatlift</a> of 1980. Cuban troops fought in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/portuguese-angola">Angola</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/federal-democratic-republic-of-ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">Sugar</a> sold to the Soviet bloc was the economic mainstay.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>Soviet collapse brought the Special Period of severe shortages in the 1990s. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raul-castro">Raúl Castro</a> succeeded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fidel-castro">Fidel</a> in 2008, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-diaz-canel">Miguel Díaz-Canel</a> became president in 2018. Mass protests in 2021, blackouts and record emigration mark the republic's current crisis.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/republic-of-cuba.jpg" alt="Republic of Cuba" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Miguel Teurbe Tolón and Narciso López · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Cuba.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1959 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Havana</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Fidel Castro, Raúl Castro, Miguel Díaz-Canel</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism and Santería</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/cuba">Cuba</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Cuba</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1992&amp;focus=republic-of-cuba" aria-label="Republic of Cuba on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2481 1324 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Republic of Cuba lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2801 1580L2801 1580"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2801 1580h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2801 1580h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2801 1580h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Havana. <a href="/atlas?year=1992&amp;focus=republic-of-cuba">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
